    "faq_mbH86F": {
      "type": "faq",
      "blocks": {
        "item_6B76QC": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "how should I introduce the mōmi bottle to my baby?",
            "content": "<p>We’ve worked closely with lactation consultants and professionals to develop the mōmi bottle, and we’ve learned a lot about feeding babies on the way. Please check out our blog for helpful tips! (“<a href=\"https://momi.baby/blogs/momtech/how-to-introduce-a-bottle-to-a-breastfed-baby\" title=\"https://momi.baby/blogs/momtech/how-to-introduce-a-bottle-to-a-breastfed-baby\">How to Introduce the mōmi Bottle to a Breastfed Baby</a>” is a great place to start, but you can search all of our feeding content with the “feeding” filter.) It sometimes takes four or five tries (or even more!) for a baby to accept a new bottle. Don’t be discouraged! And we’d love to help you troubleshoot if you run into issues with bottle introduction. Please reach out to us by email at care@momi.baby, by voicemail or text to 980-400-6455, or by chat on our website. We’ll do our very best to respond within 24 hours.</p>",
            "custom-html": ""
          }
        },
        "item_aR7VHq": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "is it normal for milk to drip or stream from the nipple?",
            "content": "<p>This can happen when warm milk heats the air inside the bottle, causing it to become pressurized. This can happen with most bottle brands.  If this is interfering with feeding, consider not warming the milk to as high a temperature. </p>",
            "custom-html": ""
          }
        },
        "item_fbkGyp": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "what if my baby chews or gnaws on the nipple?",
            "content": "<p>Some chewing or gnawing is normal when mōmi is first introduced to your baby, as babies explore the world through their mouths and they’re learning that this is a source of food. Once feeding is established, it’s important not to allow the baby to use the nipple as a pacifier or teething toy. Excessive chewing or gnawing on the nipple can cause it to wear out more quickly and can even create a safety hazard. Please see the specific safety guidelines under the “product safety” section below.</p>",
            "custom-html": ""
          }
        },
        "item_tT78k4": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "do i need to pace feed with the mōmi bottle?",
            "content": "<p>The mōmi bottle’s built-in, patented babypace® technology allows baby to regulate the flow and pace of a feeding like in nursing. No pacing needed! Your baby will be a more active participant with this bottle, which supports their healthy oral development. (Wondering what paced feeding is all about? <a href=\"https://momi.baby/blogs/momtech/all-you-need-to-know-about-paced-bottle-feeding\" title=\"https://momi.baby/blogs/momtech/all-you-need-to-know-about-paced-bottle-feeding\">Here’s everything you need to know</a>.) </p>",
            "custom-html": ""
          }
        },
        "item_YRNegV": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "what’s the best way to sanitize the mōmi bottle and nipple?",
            "content": "<p>We’ve compiled <a href=\"https://momi.baby/blogs/momtech/everything-you-need-to-know-about-cleaning-sanitizing-and-safely-storing-baby-bottles\" title=\"https://momi.baby/blogs/momtech/everything-you-need-to-know-about-cleaning-sanitizing-and-safely-storing-baby-bottles\"><span style=\"text-decoration:underline\">expert advice on washing and sanitizing baby bottles</span></a> on our blog. </p><p>For extra germ removal, we recommend sterilizing your bottle components (including nipples) daily.<br/><br/></p><p>Excessive exposure to UV, such as from a UV sterilizer or direct sunlight, may cause the silicone to decay more rapidly. For this reason, boiling is our preferred method of sterilization.<br/><br/>To sterilize by boiling:<br/>Fill a large, clean pot with enough water to cover the bottle components.</p><ul><li>Submerge the freshly washed bottle in the water upside down, making sure there aren’t any air bubbles at the bottom.</li><li>Bring the water to a boil.</li><li>Set a timer, and boil the bottle for five minutes.</li><li>Turn the heat off and remove the bottle using tongs.</li></ul><p>Place it on a clean, dry dishcloth and allow it to air dry.</p><p>Note: The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) guidelines indicate that daily sterilization may not be necessary for healthy babies older than three months. Since exposure to excessive heat and UV rays can shorten the life of silicone products, you may consider less frequent sterilization if your baby is healthy and older than three months. For more information, <a href=\"https://www.cdc.gov/healthywater/hygiene/healthychildcare/infantfeeding/cleansanitize.html\"><span style=\"text-decoration:underline\">read the CDC’s guidance here</span></a>.</p>",
            "custom-html": ""
          }
        },
        "item_kVR4ze": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "how do i clean the mōmi nipple after use?",
            "content": "<p>We recommend washing bottle components in hot sudsy water after each use.  mōmi comes with a unique cleaning tool that’s specifically designed to clean the central duct of the mōmi nipple. (Nothing should be inserted in the anticolic vent on the side of the nipple, as this can cause leaks.) Please avoid using other objects to clean the inside of the mōmi nipple as they could potentially damage the soft silicone. Wire or straw-cleaning brushes should never be used to clean your mōmi. To extend the life of your mōmi nipple, soaking is not recommended, as over time this can “cloud” or discolor the silicone. </p><p>*If you have other questions about washing, sanitizing and storing baby bottles, our blog has answers. Read “<a href=\"https://momi.baby/blogs/momtech/everything-you-need-to-know-about-cleaning-sanitizing-and-safely-storing-baby-bottles\" title=\"https://momi.baby/blogs/momtech/everything-you-need-to-know-about-cleaning-sanitizing-and-safely-storing-baby-bottles\">Everything You Need to Know About Cleaning, Sanitizing and Safely Storing Baby Bottles</a>.”</p>",
            "custom-html": ""
          }
        },
        "item_Kbd3Qt": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "how can I tell which flow rate is right for my baby?",
            "content": "<p>The mōmi babypace® nipple is available in three flow rates. </p><p><br/><strong>Important: <br/>Before using the nipple for the first time, clean it with the included cleaning tool, passing the tip of the tool through the opening of the nipple duct 5 - 10 times.  This not only cleans the duct, but also establishes the nipple's correct flow rate.  Watch a brief demo </strong><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=L0SxQwt_KNQ\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"how to use the momi nipple cleaning tool\"><strong>here.<br/></strong></a><strong><br/>Level 1</strong> is recommended for:</p><ul><li>late pre-term and full-term newborns</li><li>exclusively breastfed babies up to 3 months old who are being introduced to a bottle for the first time (unless they are accustomed to nursing with a strong letdown)</li><li>babies who have established feeding skills with other brands’ level 0 (preemie, etc.)</li><li>The Level 1 flow rate measures 11.3 mL/min with a .03 coefficient of variation (“cv”).</li></ul><p></p><p><strong>Level 2</strong> is recommended for:</p><ul><li>exclusively breastfed babies older than 3 months</li><li>exclusively breastfed babies under 3 months who have adapted to a heavy let-down</li><li>babies who have established feeding skills with other brands’ level 1 or equivalent (slow flow, etc.)</li><li>babies who have previously adapted to mōmi’s Level 1 and who have graduated to needing a faster flow rate, typically 3 months or older</li><li>Level 2 has a flow rate of 20.8 mL/min with a .04 cv.</li></ul><p><strong>Level 3</strong> is recommended for:</p><ul><li>babies who have established feeding skills with other brands’ level 2 or equivalent (medium flow, etc.), typically 6 months or older</li><li>babies who have previously adapted to mōmi’s Level 2 and who have graduated to needing a faster flow rate, typically 6 months or older</li><li>Level 3 has a flow rate of 40.0 mL/min with a .09 cv.</li></ul><p><br/>Remember that mōmi requires the baby to work for food in a way that most conventional nipples do not, so a baby who has already become accustomed to a conventional nipple may become frustrated if the selected mōmi flow rate is too slow.</p><p><br/>Every baby is unique, and sometimes exceptions to these guidelines are appropriate.  We encourage you to work with a lactation support professional if your baby is facing feeding challenges. </p>",
            "custom-html": ""
          }
        },
        "item_RTDiXe": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "how can I tell what flow rate my mōmi nipple is?",
            "content": "<p>sa</p>",
            "custom-html": "<p>Each mōmi nipple is marked with a number near the base, just to the left of the word “naturally”.</p><br>\n<img src=\"https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0539/1004/4843/files/image.png?v=1726125225\">"
          }
        },
        "item_RxNFdD": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "does the mōmi bottle work for breast milk and formula?",
            "content": "<p>Yes, the mōmi bottle can be used to feed breast milk, formula or even a mixture of both (which is even recommended when you first introduce formula). We know that every baby’s feeding journey is different!</p>",
            "custom-html": ""
          }
        },
        "item_FUbtrW": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "how do you recommend warming milk for the mōmi bottle?",
            "content": "<ul><li>Warm breast milk by placing the container of breast milk into a separate container or pot of warm water for a few minutes or by running warm (not hot) tap water over the container for a few minutes.</li><li>Do not heat breast milk directly on the stove or in the microwave.</li><li>Test the temperature of the breast milk before feeding it to your baby by putting a few drops on your wrist. It should feel warm, not hot.</li><li>Swirl the breast milk to mix the fat, which may have separated.</li><li>If your baby did not finish the bottle, the leftover breast milk can still be used within 2 hours after the baby is finished feeding. After 2 hours, leftover breast milk should be discarded.</li><li>Check out our blog for more expert advice on heating, using and storing breast milk and formula: “<a href=\"https://momi.baby/blogs/momtech/how-to-safely-store-breast-milk-and-formula-according-to-the-cdc\" title=\"https://momi.baby/blogs/momtech/how-to-safely-store-breast-milk-and-formula-according-to-the-cdc\"><span style=\"text-decoration:underline\">How to Safely Store Breast Milk and Formula According to the CDC</span></a>.” </li></ul>",
            "custom-html": ""
          }
        },
        "item_V9GpDM": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "how do you recommend warming formula for the mōmi bottle?",
            "content": "<ul><li>You do not need to warm infant formula before feeding. If you do decide to warm the formula, place the bottle with prepared formula under warm running water or into a bowl of warm water for a few minutes.</li><li>Avoid getting water into the bottle or nipple. This could contaminate the prepared formula.</li><li>Test the temperature of the formula before feeding it to your baby by putting a few drops on the inside of your wrist. It should feel warm, not hot.</li><li>Never warm infant formula in a microwave. Microwaving creates hot spots, which can burn your baby’s mouth.</li><li><a href=\"https://momi.baby/blogs/momtech/how-to-safely-store-breast-milk-and-formula-according-to-the-cdc\" title=\"https://momi.baby/blogs/momtech/how-to-safely-store-breast-milk-and-formula-according-to-the-cdc\"><span style=\"text-decoration:underline\">Read the CDC’s recommendations on safely using and storing formula</span></a>.</li></ul>",
            "custom-html": ""
          }
        },
        "item_9zGT9P": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "what if my mōmi nipple is leaking from the anticolic vent?",
            "content": "<p>This could indicate a defect. Please contact us by email at <a href=\"mailto:care@momi.baby\" title=\"mailto:care@momi.baby\">care@momi.baby</a>, by voicemail or text to 980-400-6455, or by chat here on our website. If possible, send us a photo of what you’re seeing.</p>",
            "custom-html": ""
          }
        },
        "item_Q7gF9a": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "what if my mōmi nipple “caves in” when baby is drinking from it?",
            "content": "<p>This could indicate that your baby needs a faster flow rate, or in rare cases indicate a defect. Please contact us by email at <a href=\"mailto:care@momi.baby\" title=\"mailto:care@momi.baby\">care@momi.baby</a>, by voicemail or text to 980-400-6455, or by chat here on our website. If possible, send us a photo or video of what you’re seeing.</p>",
            "custom-html": ""
          }
        },
        "item_KhGiYM": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "what if no milk seems to be coming out when my baby is trying to drink?",
            "content": "<p>First, please be sure that you have used your purple cleaning tool in the central duct of the nipple. If you have done this already and there is still no milk flow, it could indicate a defect. Please contact us by email at <a href=\"mailto:care@momi.baby\" title=\"mailto:care@momi.baby\">care@momi.baby</a>, by voicemail or text to 980-400-6455, or by chat here on our website.</p>",
            "custom-html": ""
          }
        }
      },
